        <div id="how-we-picked" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. How We Picked These Impact Drivers
          </h2>

          <p>
            We started with every brushless 1/4-inch hex impact driver currently sold by the major cordless platforms — Milwaukee, DeWalt, Makita, Ryobi, Bosch and Ridgid — and narrowed the list to tools that are widely available, current rather than discontinued, and sold as a bare tool so you can run them on batteries you already own. We left out no-name budget drivers with inflated torque claims. A 3,000 in-lb figure from a brand with no service network is not a number anyone can check, and a driver with no platform behind it is a dead end the day its battery fails.
          </p>

          <p>
            Each driver did the same set of jobs: 3-inch construction screws into doubled 2x10 framing, 1/2 x 6-inch lag screws into a 4x4 post with a pilot hole, 1-1/4-inch cabinet screws into maple face frames, and self-tapping screws into 20-gauge steel stud. The first two jobs show whether a driver has enough power. The last two show whether it has enough control, and that is where the real differences between these tools appeared.
          </p>

          <p>
            We also paid attention to things that never show up in torque charts: how far the trigger travels before the anvil starts hammering, whether the speed-mode button is easy to hit by accident, how the tool balances with a 2.0Ah pack versus a 5.0Ah pack, and whether the LED lights the screw or the back of your hand. On a tool you pick up hundreds of times during a deck build, those details decide whether you enjoy using it.
          </p>

          <p>
            We did not rank on price alone, and we did not give a pick to a driver just because it posts the biggest number. If you already own batteries for one of these platforms, the right answer is almost always that platform's driver, so our picks are organised to let you find yours quickly. Ridgid's brushless 3-speed driver is a solid tool with a strong service agreement, but it is sold mainly through Home Depot, so it is not among the cards below.
          </p>
