        <div id="torque" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Torque Figures, and Why They Don't Compare
          </h2>

          <p>
            Impact drivers are sold on torque, quoted in inch-pounds, and the numbers look precise: 1,500, 1,825, 2,000, 2,200. The problem is that there is no common test behind them. Each brand measures peak fastening torque its own way, with different joints, different fasteners, different battery packs and different rules for when the reading is taken, and then publishes the best number its method produces. Comparing a Milwaukee figure with a Ryobi figure is comparing the results of two different tests.
          </p>

          <p>
            That is how a mid-priced Ryobi ONE+ HP driver can claim 2,200 in-lbs while Milwaukee's flagship M18 FUEL claims 2,000, and how Ridgid's 3-speed driver can claim more than either. None of those figures is dishonest, exactly, but none of them tells you which tool will sink a lag faster. In our driving, the Milwaukee and DeWalt drivers finished half-inch lags quicker than the Ryobi despite their lower printed numbers, because speed under load and how well the battery delivers current matter as much as peak torque.
          </p>

          <p>
            Within a single brand, torque figures are more useful. Milwaukee rates its M12 and M18 drivers on the same basis, so the gap between the M12 FUEL 3453-20 at 1,500 in-lbs and the M18 FUEL 2953-20 at 2,000 in-lbs does mean something. It is still smaller in practice than the numbers suggest, because most driving never comes close to the top of either tool's range.
          </p>

          <p>
            The threshold that matters is lower than the marketing implies. A 3-inch deck screw into pressure-treated lumber needs a small fraction of any of these drivers' output. Structural screws and lags up to around half an inch are within reach of every conventional driver here, including the compact models, provided you drill pilot holes where the fastener maker calls for them. If you regularly drive bigger lags or break loose rusted bolts, what you need is an impact wrench with a square anvil, not a bigger impact driver.
          </p>

