        <div id="speed" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. RPM, IPM and Speed Modes
          </h2>

          <p>
            Two other numbers appear on every impact driver box. RPM is how fast the anvil spins with no load. IPM, impacts per minute, is how often the internal hammer strikes the anvil once the fastener resists. Higher RPM means a long screw spins in faster before it starts to bind; higher IPM means more frequent blows once it does. Together they explain most of the difference in driving speed you actually feel, and far more of it than peak torque does.
          </p>

          <p>
            They also explain why an impact driver can be a clumsy tool for small screws. At full speed, a driver that spins at 3,900 RPM and hammers 4,400 times a minute will sink a cabinet screw straight through a face frame or snap a brass hinge screw before you can let go of the trigger. That is why speed modes exist, and why we weighted them heavily in our picks.
          </p>

          <p>
            Most current drivers offer three or four modes. The lowest limits speed and impact rate for small fasteners; the highest is for lags and long structural screws. The useful extras are the specialised modes. Milwaukee's 4-Mode Drive Control includes a self-tapping screw mode that slows down as the screw breaks through steel. Makita's T-mode on the XDT16 reduces speed the moment the impact mechanism engages, to stop threads stripping. Ryobi's Assist mode on the PBLID02 starts slowly until the screw bites and then ramps up.
          </p>

          <p>
            Where the mode button sits matters more than you might think. Some drivers put it at the foot of the handle, where it is easy to change deliberately and hard to hit by accident. Makita puts a quick-shift button near the trigger, which we found fast once we were used to it and easy to bump before we were. Whatever you buy, get into the habit of checking the mode before driving small screws into finished work, because the cost of forgetting is a ruined face frame.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">WHAT THE NUMBERS MEAN</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">RPM</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">No-load spin speed: how fast a long screw runs in</p>
              </div>
