        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            If you already own batteries, the best impact driver is the brushless one your platform makes, and the torque race between brands should not move you. If you are starting fresh, the Milwaukee M18 FUEL 2953-20 is the strongest all-rounder we tested, the DeWalt DCF850 Atomic is the one to pick when head size matters, and the Ryobi ONE+ HP PBLID02 gives homeowners most of the performance for much less money.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Whatever you buy, spend a little on impact-rated bits, run the driver on a compact battery, and use the low speed mode for anything smaller than a deck screw. Those three habits make more difference to how the tool performs than the number printed on the box.
          </p>
